HHIISSTTOORRYY
       glibc 2.1.  C99, POSIX.1‐2001.

       POSIX.1‐2001 contains text about overflow (which might set _e_r_r_n_o to
       EERRAANNGGEE, or raise an FFEE__OOVVEERRFFLLOOWW exception).  In practice, the result
       cannot overflow on any current machine, so this error‐handling stuff
       was just nonsense.  (More precisely, overflow can happen only when the
       maximum value of the exponent is smaller than the number of mantissa
       bits.  For the IEEE‐754 standard 32‐bit and 64‐bit floating‐point
       numbers the maximum value of the exponent is 127 (respectively, 1023),
       and the number of mantissa bits including the implicit bit is 24
       (respectively, 53).)  This was removed in POSIX.1‐2008.

       If you want to store the rounded value in an integer type, you probably
       want to use one of the functions described in llrroouunndd(3) instead.

